SECTION I.
Introduction
Training a neural network with a small set of examples has always been a
challenge, since the number of trainable parameters follows a power law with
respect to the number of neurons per layer. This quickly leads to parameter
explosion in deep neural networks, with the concomitant requirement of even
more training examples to drive the learning process. Unfortunately, this is an
impeding constraint in many applications where the example data are either
scarce or hard to collect. Few-shot learning [1] [2] is a promising approach to
such problems, by building prediction models based on meta-learning principles.
Moreover, new classes can be learned without retraining, as it inherently uses
transfer learning, and verification instead of discrimination at the end. Siamese
neural networks [3] [4] have been a popular neural architecture for few-shot
learning. Still, using them also requires training, with the training data size
depending on the twin networks' complexity, and a concomitant challenge in
terms of processing resources.
In this work, we describe a Siamese neural network architecture that handles both
the network complexity and the paucity of training data, and we validate it on
molecular screening to show its performance. The next section provides a
summary of related work, Section III provides methodological background on
few-shot techniques and on the Inception neural architecture, Section IV
describes our model, section V our validation experiments, and Section VI
discusses and concludes the paper.
