SECTION II.
Related Work
Several approaches have been proposed to address the training of deep neural
networks with small training sets. Aside from helper techniques such as data
augmentation and dropout [5], there have also been efforts to create adapted
models. A summary survey follows.
Santoro et al. [6] showed that memory-augmented neural networks such as the
Neural Turing Machine [7] can learn to classify unseen before data with only a
few training examples. Using the Omniglot dataset of hand-drawn character
images [8], they show that after training by groups of five random classes, the
network could classify new data with 82.8% accuracy.
Zakharov et al. [9] perform meta-learning on a large video dataset to perform one
and few-shot learning (defined in Section III) of neural talking head models.
Using a generative adversarial network (GAN) architecture, their approach
reaches 62% prediction accuracy.
Vinyals et al. [10] develop the Matching Networks architecture to classify data
based on attention. Using the mini-ImageNet dataset, a subset of ImageNet
consisting of 60,000 color images of 100 classes, they achieve a 5-class accuracy
of 46.6% and 60% in the 1 and 5 training examples per class settings.
Gidaris and Komodakis [11] use a visual system to learn novel categories from a
few training data. They propose an attention-based classification weight generator
and redesign the CNN architecture [12], with a resulting classification accuracy of
56.20% and 73.00% on the Mini-ImageNet data set for 1 and 5 training examples
per class-shot settings.
Koch et al. [3] develop the Siamese neural network to assess the similarity
between input pairs based on distance. They generalize the predictive power of
the model to both new data and new classes from unknown distributions, leading
to 92% accuracy on the Omniglot dataset.
Puzanov and Cohen [13] propose a deep reinforcement one-shot learning
algorithm for classification. Testing it on Omniglot and on a real-world dataset for
intrusion detection led to 94% accuracy on Omniglot, and 99% for malicious
intrusion.
Altae-Tran et al. [14] combine the iterative refinement Long Short-Term Memory
(LSTM) network with graph convolutional neural networks for molecule behavior
prediction. Using three different datasets related to drug effects, they achieve


