SECTION IV.
Proposed Architecture
Fig. 1 shows the block diagram of the proposed Siamese architecture. It combines
two basic Inception modules [15] for the twin encoders-as opposed to using two
full Inception CNN networks-, a Euclidian distance computation unit and an
output classifier. The model operates in three steps: 1) The unknown input and
the class reference , both 2D images in this work, are respectively processed by
the twins to create two embedding vectors and the Euclidian
distance between the two vectors is calculated; 3) a sigmoid function converts the
obtained distance to a binary prediction: 1 if the two inputs are similar and 0
otherwise. Since a differentiable path exists between the inputs and the output
prediction, gradient descent can be used for learning the network parameters as
done for feedforward networks [12].
1) Inception Module
Fig. 2 shows the structure of each Inception encoder. The 1 x 1 convolution blocks
and the pooling block perform input space reduction and the remaining two
convolution blocks extract spatial features from the input. The convolution
mask focuses on finer-grained features in comparison to the mask. The
ReLU activation function is used at the outputs. The bypass bloc at the left of the
figure was eliminated in this work since it had no impact on the results during
our preliminary experiments, leading to an even simpler architecture.
The Inception network inputs are pixel color images, with their red, green
and blue components matrices processed according to the four parallel paths
shown in Fig. 2. Then, the results are concatenated to produce the module's
