2) Network Training
The model is trained on both positive image pairs at the input (i.e., having
structural similarities) and negative image pairs (i.e., having few or no structural
similarities). To illustrate the procedure, consider the molecular structure of the
penicillin antibiotic shown in Fig. 3. It consists of a core structure and a free
radical representing the proteins that can bind to it to create variants, hence
potentially widening the penicillin's therapeutic spectrum. This includes the
and A variants shown in Fig. 3, as well as unknown useful variants to be
discovered.
Two other molecular examples considered in this work are sulconazole and
mycophenolate. The derivatives of each one are obtained by changing one or more
atoms in the core structure. For example, Fig. 4 illustrates the structural similarity
between the mycophenolate molecule and its mycophenolate mofetil derivative.
