SECTION V.
Validation
We tested the model's efficiency with three different types of images, each one
represented by a reference. The goal was to assess the performance of the
simplified Siamese network to determine if an unseen before image bears
similarity with a reference, after training the network twins with as few examples
as possible. This is an important problem in fields such as drug discovery, where
only a few known examples are available to help the search for new drugs.
We applied our model to three arbitrarily chosen molecules from the public access
PubChem database [17], which contains more than 16 million core molecular
structures and their derivatives. The molecules were those of penicillin, the well
known antibiotic against microbial infections, sulconazole, a popular remedy for
fungal skin infections, and mycophenolate which helps prevent transplanted
organ rejection. Each of these core molecules has the potential of leading to new
therapeutic drugs by modifying it.
The images of the three molecules were used as references in the Siamese
network, and each one was paired with similar and dissimilar molecules to form
positive and negative training pairs for the twins. Thus, pairing one with its
derivatives led to positive pairs, and pairing it with one of the other two and their
derivatives led to negative pairs. We used batches of 5, 10, 25 and 50 pairs of each
kind to assess the impact of batch size on the learning process, and the data was
split 80% for training and 20% for testing. The batches were randomly shuffled
three times and the test results were averaged.
