randomly set to very small values and the learning was stopped at less
than 1000 iterations as the output stabilized then.
The simulation hardware was an entry-level PC station with an Intel i5 processor
clocked at 2.5 GHz, 8 Gb of RAM, and 64-bit Windows. No GPU accelerator was
used.
A. Results
Table 1 summarizes the obtained results. The training time varied from 9 and 86
minutes depending on batch size, with a convergence to a loss of 0.03 or less, and
an average test set prediction accuracy 95.7% for the 25-pair batch size. The
accuracy was defined as the fraction of correct predictions that a candidate
molecule is similar or dissimilar to the reference molecule. Table 1 also shows that
the results for the 5 and 10 pairs training sets were unstable. The loss function for
both cases was between 0.028 and 0.035, and the accuracy varied from 62% to
98%, possibly because of overfitting. It is also interesting to note that doubling the
batch size from 25 to 50 pairs lowered the training loss at the expense of
generalization, which usually signals overfitting.
B. Cnn Comparison
Since most reported Siamese network implementations are based on multilayer
CNNs for the twins, we also assessed the use of such networks for comparison. We
started from the VGG-16 model [18] and investigated several configurations by
changing the number and type of layers. Fig. 6 shows the one that provided the
best results, shown in Table 2. As can be seen, a lower prediction accuracy is
obtained with this network, with a higher computational cost.
