SECTION VI.
Discussion and Conclusion
Our results show that, using training sets for the twins as small as 25 positive and
negative image pairs, the proposed one-shot learning method can predict the
similarity between an unknown input and a reference with over 95% average
accuracy, at par with neural architectures that typically require much larger
training sets. We did notice, however, a degraded performance by our model when
the inputs from the test set are very different from the references. In this case, it
may be appropriate to create a new reference to compare to, instead of having to
retrain the Siamese as would be with a classical neural network architecture.
The obtained results confirm the idea that the Siamese network's twins only need
to create relevant embeddings of the input data for efficient few-shot learning,
hence mitigating the need to use complex neural networks to implement them.
The Inception module shown in Figure 2 may also undergo further simplification
for black and white images. In this case, the 1 x 1 convolution blocks can be
eliminated for even faster processing and lighter model implementation. Finally,
our model's relative simplicity makes it fit for many edge-computing applications,
including fine tuning in real time. In a previous work [25], an earlier model with
CNN twins was used to tune the prediction of hand gestures from
electromyographic measurements for different users. A GPU accelerator was used
to implement the model, but the light architecture described here can be
implemented with an ARM or RISC-V CPU for better energy consumption and
lower cost. A custom RISK-V-based embedded system is under development.
